{
  "description": "WS6.1 skill-equivalence calibration set, v3 (PRIMARY). Keyword vs keyword, short terms on both sides, matching the real runtime shape: CV skills[] and vacancy requiredSkills are both flat arrays of short terms, not sentences (WS4 schema, 5.4/5.5). This is what actually sets SKILL_SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD. v2 used full descriptive phrases for true_match (2-5 words) against 1-2 word hard_negative/true_non_match pairs — a genre mismatch that structurally favours the near-identical short hard negatives regardless of true relatedness, and produced a 100% false-negative rate that measured the mismatch, not the model. This set fixes the shape; see skills-secondary-pairs.json for the phrase-vs-keyword (category-to-instance) case, kept as a separate, non-threshold-setting, informational measurement. true_match still mixes 'equivalence' and 'category_instance' relations per 5.4 design decision (b) — both matter, both now length-matched to hard_negative/true_non_match.",
  "categories": {
    "true_match": "Genuine semantic equivalence at the embedding layer, short terms both sides — never an abbreviation or surface variant (those are layer 1/2, tested separately in normalization-canonicalization-pairs.json). Similarity should be HIGH.",
    "true_non_match": "Genuinely unrelated skills. Similarity should be LOW.",
    "hard_negative": "Lexically or contextually close, but NOT the same skill. The threshold must reject these while still accepting true_match. Java/JavaScript is the litmus test (5.4/6.1)."
  },
